  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1059 PM MDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nCONTINUED COOL THIS WEEKEND WITH WARMUP INTO EARLY THIS WEEK:\n\nClouds are beginning to increase from the southwest this evening\nas the high pressure shifts eastward allowing some moisture to\nwork in the southwest flow. A weak shortwave disturbance will\ntrack through the Four Corners Sunday allowing for some isolated\nlight showers to form over the San Juans. This continues into\nMonday with better coverage of showers across the central and\nsouthern mountains. Very little in the way of forcing will lend\nitself to light precipitation amounts with a dusting of snow\npossible above treeline in the San Juans. Temperatures will near\nnormal Sunday with a bit of WAA heading into Tuesday and\nWednesday ahead of a deep closed low that will dive down the\nwest coast and close off over SoCal. \n\nWET WEEK AHEAD:\n\nHigh pressure will be positioned to the southeast over Texas\nwith the low off the SoCal coast, allowing deeper moisture to\nadvect northward through southeast Utah and southwest Colorado\nWednesday through Thursday. PWAT anomalies climb towards 300\npercent of normal during this time, which equates to PWAT of\n0.75 to 1 inch across much of the area with PWAT above an inch\nover the Four Corners. Showers become more widespread Wednesday\nand Thursday in this moist southwest flow with potential for\nmore substantial rainfall for southern Colorado and southeast\nUtah. There is also potential for mountain snowfall but it all\ndepends on how much cold air arrives with this low pressure\nsystem. Models have not been in great agreement on the depth of\ncold air and seem to be trending milder as this low lifts across\nthe Great Basin and into the northern Rockies Thursday night\ninto Friday, with the coldest air now across Montana and\nWyoming. Much cooler temperatures arrive on the backside of this\nlow Friday into the coming weekend with unsettled conditions in\nthe northwest flow through next Saturday for the mountains.\nStill a lot of details to work out in the coming days with this\nsystem in terms of precipitation amounts and mountain snowfall\ngiven the uncertainty with how much cold air arrives with this\nsystem. Regardless, it looks to be a wet week ahead.",
